"Belmont is an elevated L station on the North Side, its platform an island between two tracks."
"Blue signs on the roof beams read BELMONT and Red Line in white letters."
"A lit board overhead scrolls train times in orange: Howard one way, 95th/Dan Ryan the other."
"A narrow glass shelter on the platform has a bench and three heat lamps in its roof."
"A button on a post, marked PUSH FOR HEAT, lights the lamps for a short while before they click off."
"Snow falls past the platform lights, and brick backs of buildings with dark windows face the tracks."
"A backlit poster in the shelter advertises the Anesidora Compassion Fund for victims of superhumans."
"A stair at the shelter's end, white with snow, leads down to the fare turnstiles and Belmont Avenue."
"Before dawn on a Saturday the platform is nearly empty, and trains call every ten minutes or so."
"A man in a puffy coat and knit cap waits at the far end, looking at his phone."
"The man is a line cook going to an early shift, tired, decent, and wary of strangers."
"Bare feet on the snowy stairs or street go numb within minutes and risk frostbite within the hour."
"Riding a train takes a fare card or a phone tapped at the turnstile below."
